                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your home does have a fairly large amount of air leakage, and there are opportunities to improve. I did note some air leakage around the two portholes in your wall, as well as some at the corners of your house. I also noted that there was some leakage around the exterior door in your basement. 



I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR® rated model, if you have not done so already.

Other upgrades recommended in this report may also affect your air leakage rate. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $300&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



This type of renovation is typically done at the same time as a siding replacement, and at this time your siding is in good shape. If you are not considering this upgrade in the immediate future, we recommend contacting the Arctic Energy Alliance once you are ready to upgrade your wall insulation for up-to-date construction and rebate information. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $650&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;19.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



</mainWalls>
                <exposedFloors>

The exposed floor of the window bench in your living room appear to be 2x8 joists with 18 inches (R28) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation. 



Because the area under the bench is so small, you would not be eligible for a rebate for adding additional insulation. However, as you indicated that this area is cold, I do recommend you consider adding additional insulation to the underside of the floor. I also recommend some air sealing work in this area.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $2.00&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. You are not eligible for a rebate under the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program as the area you would be insulating is less than 14 square metres.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate your entire exposed floor and a minimum floor area of 14 square metres (150 square feet) must be insulated. You must also increase the insulation value by a minimum of RSI 3.5 (R-20). You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</exposedFloors>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with 4 inches of insulation. I have not modelled adding any insulation to these walls. 



Your basement pony walls appear to be 2x6 framing with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled continuing the 3 inches of rigid foam insulation recommended for your main walls all the way down to ground level. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam polyisocyanurate insulation to the pony walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $230&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$366 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</foundation>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have an electric hot water tank, that was installed in 2017. You could replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $1,090&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 83% AFUE. As an option, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency propane furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion propane-fired furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $2230&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;51.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Another option is to upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency oil furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 87% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $820&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;24.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Your propane fireplace is currently an unsealed unit, with a pilot light. I recommend that you turn the pilot light off whenever you are not using the fireplace. I also recommend that if you are planning on using the fireplace frequently, that you upgrade it to a sealed fireplace with a spark ignition. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your propane fireplace with a sealed combustion propane fireplace with spark ignition, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $820&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are no rebates for this upgrade under the GNWT Energy Incentive Program.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely. The short and dark days of winter provide little or no usable energy, depending on the latitude in the NWT. However, the long hours of sunlight in spring and summer can provide usable heat and electricity for up to eight months of the year.  As prices continue to drop, solar PV panels are becoming competitive with the cost of diesel generation. 



The estimated savings here represent an estimate of the electricity a solar PV system could generate over the course of one year. They do not take into account shading from trees, power lines, or other houses. The Arctic Energy Alliance strongly recommends that you speak to a solar installer if you are considering a solar PV system, to confirm that such a system would be feasible. 



I have modelled a 3kW system on the south facing wall of your house, as neither roof section is ideal for solar. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3kW solar photovoltaic system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $950&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;11.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;.



&lt;b&gt; As you live in a community that uses hydroelectricity, you are not eligible for a rebate under the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program.  



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>



All of your windows are in good shape, and I do not recommend upgrading them at this time. Should you need to replace any of them for any reason, look for ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



ADDITIONAL COMMENTS

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $18.70 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

All prices are including GST. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



